<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Preference-based alignment methods (e.g., RLHF, DPO) typically optimize a single scalar objective, implicitly averaging over heterogeneous human preferences. In practice, systematic annotator and user-group disagreement makes mean-reward maximization brittle and susceptible to proxy over-optimization. We propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding (DARC)</span>, a retraining-free inference-time method that frames response selection as distributionally robust, risk-sensitive decision making. Given multiple preference samples or scalable disagreement proxies, DARC reranks candidates by maximizing a <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">KL-robust (entropic)</em> satisfaction objective, and provides simple deployment controls that cap or penalize the corresponding entropic risk premium relative to the mean, enabling explicit risk budgets without retraining. We provide theoretical characterization linking this decoding rule to principled pessimism and KL-based distributionally robust optimization. Experiments on alignment benchmarks show that DARC reduces disagreement and tail risk while maintaining competitive average quality under noisy, heterogeneous feedback.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Machine Learning, ICML
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.08145v1/ridge_plot_icml_style_new.svg" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/359;" width="476" height="359"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S0.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Score Distribution shift.<span id="S0.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Ridge plot showing human score densities on the high-disagreement subset. DARC variants (blue) shift the distribution to the right (higher mean <math id="S0.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>) compared to the baseline (grey), with reduced spread (lower <math id="S0.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating both increased satisfaction and reduced disagreement.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Preference data has become the dominant supervision signal for aligning large language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stiennon et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
Most pipelines—RLHF with reward modeling and RL optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite>,
offline preference objectives such as DPO and its refinements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>,
and reference-free single-stage variants such as ORPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>—share a common abstraction:
preferences are treated as noisy observations of a <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single latent scalar utility</em> (e.g., Bradley–Terry) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bradley and Terry, 1952</a>)</cite>.
This abstraction largely persists in newer reformulations such as KTO and IPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ethayarajh et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garg et al., 2025</a>)</cite>,
and even when reward models are made multi-dimensional via multi-head/objective designs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Yet, treating feedback as perturbations around a single scalar provides limited guidance for <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inference-time</em> response selection under heterogeneous preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hung et al., 2025</a>)</cite>,
and it also lacks a unifying robust-optimization account for common risk-penalized decoding heuristics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, real-world preferences are often <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">heterogeneous</em> rather than i.i.d. noise:
annotators disagree for systematic reasons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Empirically, human ratings show substantial variance even on the raw Top-<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> candidate pool (Appendix <a href="#A8.SS1" title="H.1 Human disagreement on the raw Top-𝐾 candidate pool ‣ Appendix H Experiments ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.1</span></a>),
suggesting that uncertainty is intrinsic rather than an artifact of our selection rule.
Under such plurality, maximizing the <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">average</em> reward <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> can be brittle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Casper et al., 2023</a>)</cite>,
and the issue is exacerbated by proxy over-optimization, which can improve an imperfect preference proxy while degrading the underlying target <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work further shows that proxy misspecification can induce <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inference-time</em> reward hacking:
as best-of-<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (a widely used decoding primitive <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite>) or soft best-of-<math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> becomes greedier,
true utility can increase and then inevitably degrade <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.
Best-of-Poisson and HedgeTune mitigate this effect by tuning inference-time parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khalaf et al., 2025</a>)</cite>;
however, they primarily model risk through proxy–distortion tradeoffs under a <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single</em> reward signal, rather than preference heterogeneity.
Closely related, pessimistic best-of-<math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> rules penalize atypical candidates via an auxiliary error model to mitigate reward hacking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anonymous, 2025</a>)</cite>,
but they target distributional uncertainty of the reward model (e.g., atypicality/OOD) rather than disagreement-grounded risk across users.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In parallel, robustness has been pursued through <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">training-time</em> objectives such as robust DPO under noisy preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>,
which improve robustness via retraining and noise assumptions;
group-robust objectives that protect minority preference groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramesh et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which rely on access to group structure;
and uncertainty-aware reward modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Banerjee and Gopalan, 2024</a>)</cite>, which quantifies estimation uncertainty but does not by itself specify how to select responses under inference-time proxy shift <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ichihara et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Taken together, these lines suggest a common lesson:
when preferences are plural, the relevant object is not a deterministic score, but a <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">random variable</em> over users and annotation noise.
Yet, a principled <em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inference-time</em> selection rule that is explicitly risk-constrained under heterogeneous preferences remains underdeveloped.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We therefore study inference-time alignment under heterogeneous preferences through the lens of <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">risk-constrained decision making</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chow et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tamar et al., 2015</a>)</cite>.
Given a fixed candidate set and noisy preference or reward scores, we derive a finite-sample <em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pessimistic</em> rule based on a lower confidence bound,
yielding high-probability guarantees for selecting a competitive response while controlling tail risk across prompts.
This leads to <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding (DARC)</span>, an inference-time-only, retraining-free procedure that plugs into any LM and preference estimator.
DARC grounds risk in <em id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multi-annotator disagreement</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> instantiated via validated proxy signals,
improving robustness on high-disagreement prompts (Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
We include representative cases where Best-of-<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is polarizing or unstable, whereas DARC yields consistently preferred responses (Appendix <a href="#A8.SS10" title="H.10 Representative cases ‣ Appendix H Experiments ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.10</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond the statistical view, we give a distributionally robust optimization (DRO) characterization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wiesemann et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rahimian and Mehrotra, 2019</a>)</cite>,
viewing decoding as maximizing the worst-case expected satisfaction over local divergence neighborhoods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Namkoong and Duchi, 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duchi et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
This yields a practical KL-robust instantiation and situates widely used mean–dispersion scoring rules within the same DRO perspective,
clarifying the conditions under which they arise as principled risk-sensitive criteria <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duchi and Namkoong, 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contributions.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method.</span> We formulate inference-time alignment as <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">risk-constrained</em> decision making under heterogeneous preferences, with risk induced by preference uncertainty and annotator disagreement.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theory.</span> We connect LCB-based uniform pessimism to a KL-DRO view, yielding a closed-form entropic decoding objective and its constrained/penalized variants via an entropic risk premium.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirics.</span> Across benchmarks, DARC improves disagreement and prompt-level tail risk with competitive mean quality; a <em id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dual-robust</em> multi-scorer extension hedges scorer shift and proxy over-optimization, with a KL-regularized DRO interpretation over scorers.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Problem setup</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> denote a prompt (context) and let <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> be a realized candidate set produced by a fixed generator (e.g., sampling, beam variants, or a proposal model), with <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:=|\mathcal{Y}(s)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:=|\mathcal{Y}(s)|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conditioning on the candidate set.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The generator may be stochastic; throughout the analysis we condition on the realized <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>.
All probabilities below are taken over the evaluation randomness (human or otherwise), holding <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> fixed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Latent satisfaction under heterogeneous preferences.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(s,y)\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(s,y)\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a (latent) user-satisfaction random variable capturing preference heterogeneity and evaluation noise, with mean <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(s,y):=\mathbb{E}[R(s,y)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(s,y):=\mathbb{E}[R(s,y)]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Intuitively, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(s,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(s,y)</annotation></semantics></math> measures average quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">KL-robust (entropic) value and risk premium.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, define the entropic value</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\beta}(s,y):=-\frac{1}{\beta}\log\mathbb{E}\!\left[\exp\!\big(-\beta\,R(s,y)\big)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\beta}(s,y):=-\frac{1}{\beta}\log\mathbb{E}\!\left[\exp\!\big(-\beta\,R(s,y)\big)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is equivalent to a KL-based distributionally robust objective (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Distributional risk: DRO characterizations of pessimistic value ‣ 3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).
We define the entropic risk premium.</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{RP}_{\beta}(s,y):=\mu(s,y)-V_{\beta}(s,y)\geq 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>RP</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{RP}_{\beta}(s,y):=\mu(s,y)-V_{\beta}(s,y)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Decision problem (risk-aware decoding).</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conditioning on <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,\mathcal{Y}(s))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,\mathcal{Y}(s))</annotation></semantics></math>, each candidate <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> induces an (unknown) satisfaction distribution over users/raters.
Our population objective is to select an output by solving</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\star}\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}V_{\beta}(s,y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\star}\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}V_{\beta}(s,y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where entropic risk measure <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ KL-robust (entropic) value and risk premium. ‣ 2 Problem setup ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
We further consider explicit risk control via a budget or a penalty:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\star}_{\tau}\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}V_{\beta}(s,y)\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\mathrm{RP}_{\beta}(s,y)\leq\tau," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>RP</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\star}_{\tau}\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}V_{\beta}(s,y)\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\mathrm{RP}_{\beta}(s,y)\leq\tau,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">or in penalized (Lagrangian) form</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}V_{\beta}(s,y)-\lambda\,\mathrm{RP}_{\beta}(s,y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>RP</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}V_{\beta}(s,y)-\lambda\,\mathrm{RP}_{\beta}(s,y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides a statistical justification for disagreement-aware decoding by deriving high-probability lower confidence bounds (LCBs) on expected satisfaction under heterogeneous preferences.
To contextualize the resulting pessimistic rules, we also give complementary distributionally robust optimization (DRO) characterizations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scalar satisfaction samples (guarantee setting).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each candidate <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>, we observe <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. scalar satisfaction samples
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{R_{i}(s,y)\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{R_{i}(s,y)\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from the (unknown) distribution of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(s,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(s,y)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}(s,y)\overset{i.i.d.}{\sim}R(s,y),\qquad i=1,\ldots,n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">.</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}(s,y)\overset{i.i.d.}{\sim}R(s,y),\qquad i=1,\ldots,n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In this regime, the empirical mean and standard deviation
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{n}(s,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{n}(s,y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{n}(s,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{n}(s,y)</annotation></semantics></math> estimate <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(s,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(s,y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(s,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(s,y)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, the plug-in estimator of (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ KL-robust (entropic) value and risk premium. ‣ 2 Problem setup ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}_{\beta}(s,y):=-\frac{1}{\beta}\log\!\left(\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\exp\!\big(-\beta\,R_{i}(s,y)\big)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.146em;" width="1.146em"><mi>log</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}_{\beta}(s,y):=-\frac{1}{\beta}\log\!\left(\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\exp\!\big(-\beta\,R_{i}(s,y)\big)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with the empirical risk premium</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathrm{RP}}_{\beta}(s,y):=\hat{\mu}_{n}(s,y)-\widehat{V}_{\beta}(s,y)\ \geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>RP</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathrm{RP}}_{\beta}(s,y):=\hat{\mu}_{n}(s,y)-\widehat{V}_{\beta}(s,y)\ \geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">In practice, one may operate with approximate (proxy) estimates of these empirical quantities; the analysis below does not rely on proxy scores being independent, and any such approximation induces only an additive slack in the resulting LCB objective (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS12" title="A.12 Robustness to proxy disagreement estimates ‣ Appendix A Additional Theoretical Details ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.12</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Estimation risk: uniform LCB and a mean–dispersion surrogate</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix a prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and condition on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\mathcal{Y}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\mathcal{Y}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:=|\mathcal{Y}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:=|\mathcal{Y}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
For readability, write <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}(s,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}(s,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(y),\sigma(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(y),\sigma(y)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(s,y),\sigma(s,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(s,y),\sigma(s,y)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, we observe <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> independent satisfaction samples with mean <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(y)</annotation></semantics></math> and variance <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and denote the empirical mean and standard deviation by
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{n}(y):=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}R_{i}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{n}(y):=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}R_{i}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{n}^{2}(y):=\frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(R_{i}(y)-\hat{\mu}_{n}(y))^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{n}^{2}(y):=\frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(R_{i}(y)-\hat{\mu}_{n}(y))^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bridge to pairwise preferences.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our scalar-sample analysis can be viewed as operating on standard scalarizations of pairwise preferences (e.g., win-rate or fitted BT/Thurstone scores); see Appendix <a href="#A1.SS14" title="A.14 From pairwise comparisons to scalar samples ‣ Appendix A Additional Theoretical Details ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.14</span></a>. Empirically, our main conclusions are stable when replacing absolute scalar ratings with a pairwise scalarization against the base response (Table <a href="#A8.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Prompt-level stability across scalarizations. ‣ H.5 Scalarization robustness: absolute ratings vs. pairwise preferences ‣ Appendix H Experiments ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>(Shared annotators across candidates)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our guarantee requires independence across <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">for each fixed candidate <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math></em>.
Annotator overlap <em id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">across candidates</em> may induce dependence between <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\hat{\mu}_{n}(y),\hat{\sigma}_{n}(y))\}_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\hat{\mu}_{n}(y),\hat{\sigma}_{n}(y))\}_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
but the uniform guarantee below follows from per-candidate concentration with a union bound and does not require independence across candidates.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Bounded rewards)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, the satisfaction samples are almost surely bounded:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}(y)\in[a,b]\quad\text{a.s.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>a.s.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}(y)\in[a,b]\quad\text{a.s.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Boundedness matches typical rating scales and can be enforced for proxy scores via truncation (Appendix H.1); an analogous LCB holds under sub-Gaussian noise (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Lower-tail (risk) interpretation and uniform empirical LCB under sub-Gaussianity ‣ Appendix A Additional Theoretical Details ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Uniform LCB)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists an absolute constant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability at least <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, simultaneously for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(y)\geq\hat{\mu}_{n}(y)-c\,\hat{\sigma}_{n}(y)\sqrt{\frac{\log(K/\delta)}{n}}-c\,(b-a)\frac{\log(K/\delta)}{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(y)\geq\hat{\mu}_{n}(y)-c\,\hat{\sigma}_{n}(y)\sqrt{\frac{\log(K/\delta)}{n}}-c\,(b-a)\frac{\log(K/\delta)}{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We denote the right-hand side by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LCB}_{\delta}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>LCB</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LCB}_{\delta}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof.</span> See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix A Additional Theoretical Details ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>(Variance governs estimation hardness)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LCB form (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Proposition 3.3 (Uniform LCB). ‣ Bridge to pairwise preferences. ‣ 3.1 Estimation risk: uniform LCB and a mean–dispersion surrogate ‣ 3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) highlights a statistical driver of selection risk: the dominant estimation error scales with the standard deviation <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(y)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The lower-order term decays as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/n)</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the leading term scales as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\sigma(y)/\sqrt{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\sigma(y)/\sqrt{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, low-disagreement candidates (small <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(y)</annotation></semantics></math>) admit substantially tighter confidence bounds at the same sample size, while controversial candidates require many more samples to certify their mean.
Penalizing <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{n}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{n}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> therefore has an identification rationale: it discourages selecting candidates whose true quality is intrinsically harder to verify from limited feedback.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Lower-tail interpretation.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Maximizing a lower confidence bound is a principled way to avoid candidates with poor lower-tail satisfaction even when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{n}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{n}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> is high <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boucheron et al., 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vershynin, 2018</a>)</cite>.
This is a statistical conservatism argument rather than an equivalence to coherent tail-risk measures such as CVaR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Artzner et al., 1999</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rockafellar et al., 2000</a>)</cite>.
See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Lower-tail (risk) interpretation and uniform empirical LCB under sub-Gaussianity ‣ Appendix A Additional Theoretical Details ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> for details.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">On constants and practical calibration.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The uniform LCB in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Proposition 3.3 (Uniform LCB). ‣ Bridge to pairwise preferences. ‣ 3.1 Estimation risk: uniform LCB and a mean–dispersion surrogate ‣ 3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) is obtained via standard concentration plus a union bound over <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> candidates and is therefore conservative.
We do not claim tight constants; instead, the bound motivates the <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">functional form</em></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\delta}\;\propto\;\sqrt{\frac{\log(K/\delta)}{n}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">∝</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\delta}\;\propto\;\sqrt{\frac{\log(K/\delta)}{n}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the lower-order term <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(b-a)\tfrac{\log(K/\delta)}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(b-a)\tfrac{\log(K/\delta)}{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniform across candidates and does not affect <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arg\max_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arg\max_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> decisions.
In practice, we treat the coefficient as a risk-budget knob (optionally scaled by a factor <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) and fix it via a small held-out calibration, while reporting sensitivity of the resulting trade-off. Combined with the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{n}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{n}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> factor in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Proposition 3.3 (Uniform LCB). ‣ Bridge to pairwise preferences. ‣ 3.1 Estimation risk: uniform LCB and a mean–dispersion surrogate ‣ 3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), the uncertainty penalty scales as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(\hat{\sigma}_{n}(y)\sqrt{\log K/n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(\hat{\sigma}_{n}(y)\sqrt{\log K/n})</annotation></semantics></math>, matching the intuition that more controversial candidates require larger risk budgets to be selected.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LCB decoding and a mean–dispersion surrogate.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the LCB decoder as</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\mathrm{LCB}}\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\mathrm{LCB}_{\delta}(y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>LCB</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>LCB</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\mathrm{LCB}}\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\mathrm{LCB}_{\delta}(y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">On the high-probability event of Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3 (Uniform LCB). ‣ Bridge to pairwise preferences. ‣ 3.1 Estimation risk: uniform LCB and a mean–dispersion surrogate ‣ 3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, this rule maximizes a valid lower bound on the true mean satisfaction <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(y)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Crucially, under bounded ratings, maximizing <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LCB}_{\delta}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>LCB</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LCB}_{\delta}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent (up to a constant) to a <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mean–dispersion</em> surrogate:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\big(\hat{\mu}_{n}(y)-\lambda\hat{\sigma}_{n}(y)\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\big(\hat{\mu}_{n}(y)-\lambda\hat{\sigma}_{n}(y)\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We provide the derivation in Corollary <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary A.2 (Mean–dispersion surrogate form under bounded ratings). ‣ A.3 From LCB decoding to mean–dispersion decoding ‣ Appendix A Additional Theoretical Details ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 From LCB decoding to mean–dispersion decoding ‣ Appendix A Additional Theoretical Details ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>) and discuss the theoretical connection between this <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-penalty (estimation uncertainty) and the entropic <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-penalty (risk aversion) in Remark <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem3" title="Remark A.3 (Standard deviation vs. Variance penalization). ‣ A.3 From LCB decoding to mean–dispersion decoding ‣ Appendix A Additional Theoretical Details ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Distributional risk: DRO characterizations of pessimistic value</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">KL-robust (entropic) decoding.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first consider a relative-entropy (KL) robustification of expected satisfaction.
Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be the (latent) satisfaction with reference distribution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We consider worst-case distributions <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> that are absolutely continuous w.r.t. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, define the KL-regularized robust value</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{Rob}^{\mathrm{KL}}_{\beta}(\mathbb{P};R):=\inf_{\mathbb{Q}\ll\mathbb{P}}\Big\{\mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{Q}}[R]+\beta^{-1}D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathbb{Q}\|\mathbb{P})\Big\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Rob</mi><mi>β</mi><mi>KL</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Rob}^{\mathrm{KL}}_{\beta}(\mathbb{P};R):=\inf_{\mathbb{Q}\ll\mathbb{P}}\Big\{\mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{Q}}[R]+\beta^{-1}D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathbb{Q}\|\mathbb{P})\Big\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}\ll\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}\ll\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> ensures the KL term is well-defined.
This is standard in risk-sensitive control and large deviations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dupuis and Ellis, 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hansen and Sargent, 2011</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(KL-robust value equals an entropic objective)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}}[\exp(-\beta R)]&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}}[\exp(-\beta R)]&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Rob}^{\mathrm{KL}}_{\beta}(\mathbb{P};R)\;=\;-\frac{1}{\beta}\log\mathbb{E}_{R\sim\mathbb{P}}\!\left[\exp(-\beta R)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Rob</mi><mi>β</mi><mi>KL</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Rob}^{\mathrm{KL}}_{\beta}(\mathbb{P};R)\;=\;-\frac{1}{\beta}\log\mathbb{E}_{R\sim\mathbb{P}}\!\left[\exp(-\beta R)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, for the empirical rater distribution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbb{P}}_{n}^{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mi>y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbb{P}}_{n}^{y}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Rob}^{\mathrm{KL}}_{\beta}(\widehat{\mathbb{P}}_{n}^{y};R)=-\frac{1}{\beta}\log\!\big(\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\exp(-\beta R_{i}(y))\big)=\widehat{V}_{\beta}(s,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Rob</mi><mi>β</mi><mi>KL</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mi>y</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.146em;" width="1.146em"><mi>log</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Rob}^{\mathrm{KL}}_{\beta}(\widehat{\mathbb{P}}_{n}^{y};R)=-\frac{1}{\beta}\log\!\big(\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\exp(-\beta R_{i}(y))\big)=\widehat{V}_{\beta}(s,y)</annotation></semantics></math>.See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Additional Theoretical Details ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a> for the detailed proof.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Relation to Expected Utility Theory (CARA).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Maximizing the entropic value in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ Theorem 3.5 (KL-robust value equals an entropic objective). ‣ KL-robust (entropic) decoding. ‣ 3.2 Distributional risk: DRO characterizations of pessimistic value ‣ 3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) is equivalent to maximizing expected utility under a <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">constant absolute risk aversion</em> (CARA) utility, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(x)=-\exp(-\beta x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(x)=-\exp(-\beta x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
A useful invariance is <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">translation equivariance</em>: for any constant <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\beta}(R+c)=V_{\beta}(R)+c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\beta}(R+c)=V_{\beta}(R)+c</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the risk premium <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(R)-V_{\beta}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(R)-V_{\beta}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant to additive reward shifts.
This is desirable in RLHF-style pipelines where learned reward models are often only identifiable up to an affine transformation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-DRO yields a mean–dispersion special case.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now give a complementary robust-optimization view <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wiesemann et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rahimian and Mehrotra, 2019</a>)</cite>:
under a <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence ambiguity set around <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ben-Tal and Nemirovski, 2002</a>)</cite>,
the worst-case expected satisfaction admits a mean–dispersion form.
(Definitions and tightness conditions are deferred to Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of Proposition (𝜒^2-DRO robust mean) ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-DRO robust mean admits a mean–dispersion form)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be square-integrable under <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> with mean <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathbb{P}}:=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}}[R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathbb{P}}:=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}}[R]</annotation></semantics></math>
and variance <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\mathbb{P}}^{2}:=\mathrm{Var}_{\mathbb{P}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Var</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\mathbb{P}}^{2}:=\mathrm{Var}_{\mathbb{P}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{\mathbb{Q}\in\mathcal{U}_{\rho}(\mathbb{P})}\ \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{Q}}[R]\;\geq\;\mu_{\mathbb{P}}\;-\;\sqrt{\rho}\,\sigma_{\mathbb{P}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>ρ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{\mathbb{Q}\in\mathcal{U}_{\rho}(\mathbb{P})}\ \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{Q}}[R]\;\geq\;\mu_{\mathbb{P}}\;-\;\sqrt{\rho}\,\sigma_{\mathbb{P}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Specializing to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}=\widehat{\mathbb{P}}_{n}^{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mi>y</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}=\widehat{\mathbb{P}}_{n}^{y}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the empirical closed form</span></p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{\mathbb{Q}\in\mathcal{U}_{\rho}(\widehat{\mathbb{P}}_{n}^{y})}\ \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{Q}}[R]=\widehat{\mu}_{n}(y)-\sqrt{\rho}\,\sqrt{\widehat{v}_{n}(y)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mi>y</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>ρ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{\mathbb{Q}\in\mathcal{U}_{\rho}(\widehat{\mathbb{P}}_{n}^{y})}\ \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{Q}}[R]=\widehat{\mu}_{n}(y)-\sqrt{\rho}\,\sqrt{\widehat{v}_{n}(y)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">whenever the extremal density is nonnegative on the empirical support. Otherwise, the mean–dispersion form remains a valid lower bound (Remark <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Remark B.1 (Conservative approximation under gap). ‣ B.3 Specialization to the empirical distribution ‣ Appendix B Proof of Proposition (𝜒^2-DRO robust mean) ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>), ensuring the rule remains pessimistic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof and tightness conditions.</span>
See Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of Proposition (𝜒^2-DRO robust mean) ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for the extremal density characterization and a sufficient nonnegativity regime (e.g., bounded ratings imply tightness for small <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">A unified pessimistic value (LCB as calibrated DRO).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define the candidate-wise pessimistic value</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\delta}(y):=\widehat{\mu}_{n}(y)-c\,\widehat{\sigma}_{n}(y)\sqrt{\frac{\log(K/\delta)}{n}}-c\,(b-a)\frac{\log(K/\delta)}{n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\delta}(y):=\widehat{\mu}_{n}(y)-c\,\widehat{\sigma}_{n}(y)\sqrt{\frac{\log(K/\delta)}{n}}-c\,(b-a)\frac{\log(K/\delta)}{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is a uniform lower confidence bound (LCB) under Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3 (Uniform LCB). ‣ Bridge to pairwise preferences. ‣ 3.1 Estimation risk: uniform LCB and a mean–dispersion surrogate ‣ 3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix A Additional Theoretical Details ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>).
On the other hand, Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6 (𝜒^2-DRO robust mean admits a mean–dispersion form). ‣ 𝜒^2-DRO yields a mean–dispersion special case. ‣ 3.2 Distributional risk: DRO characterizations of pessimistic value ‣ 3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> implies that, up to the lower-order term in (<a href="#S3.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ A unified pessimistic value (LCB as calibrated DRO). ‣ 3.2 Distributional risk: DRO characterizations of pessimistic value ‣ 3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>),
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\delta}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\delta}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> matches the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-DRO robust mean of the empirical rater distribution with radius</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\delta}\asymp\frac{\log(K/\delta)}{n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>≍</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\delta}\asymp\frac{\log(K/\delta)}{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">since <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\rho_{\delta}}\,\widehat{\sigma}_{n}(y)\asymp\widehat{\sigma}_{n}(y)\sqrt{\log(K/\delta)/n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≍</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\rho_{\delta}}\,\widehat{\sigma}_{n}(y)\asymp\widehat{\sigma}_{n}(y)\sqrt{\log(K/\delta)/n}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Calibrating LCB scaling to 𝜒^2-DRO radius ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> for a discussion distinguishing statistical estimation risk from intrinsic disagreement).
This yields a unified interpretation of disagreement-aware decoding as <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">calibrated pessimism</em>:a statistical LCB rule that is equivalent (up to lower-order terms) to optimizing a local DRO objective. Thus, disagreement-aware decoding can be viewed equivalently as
(i) maximizing a high-probability LCB on expected satisfaction (statistical pessimism),
or (ii) maximizing a distributionally robust worst-case expected satisfaction over a local <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> neighborhood (adversarial robustness).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Disagreement-Aware Risk-Constrained Decoding</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the LCB and DRO characterization in §<a href="#S3" title="3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>–§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Distributional risk: DRO characterizations of pessimistic value ‣ 3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we now present practical decoding rules that implement the <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">KL-robust entropic objective</em> (and its constrained/penalized variants) over a finite candidate set.
For completeness, we also include a <em id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">second-moment</em> pessimistic surrogate implied by the LCB analysis in (<a href="#A1.E42" title="Equation 42 ‣ Corollary A.9 (Second-moment surrogate). ‣ A.11 Second-moment surrogate of the LCB rule ‣ Appendix A Additional Theoretical Details ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>) as a computationally convenient ablation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>From preference maximization to risk controls</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Standard reward-based reranking selects the highest estimated mean,</p>
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{mean}}(s)\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}\hat{\mu}(s,y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>mean</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{mean}}(s)\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}\hat{\mu}(s,y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which optimizes <em id="S4.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">average</em> preference but may favor brittle outputs when disagreement is large.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Risk-sensitive decoding (primary rule).</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our primary decoder selects the candidate with the largest <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">scorer-robust</em> entropic value:</p>
<table id="S4.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{Entropic}}(s)\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}\widehat{V}_{\beta}(s,y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>Entropic</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{Entropic}}(s)\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}\widehat{V}_{\beta}(s,y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is the entropic value defined in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Scalar satisfaction samples (guarantee setting). ‣ 3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Risk-constrained decoding via an entropic risk premium.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define explicit deployment knobs using the entropic risk premium
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathrm{RP}}_{\beta}(s,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>RP</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathrm{RP}}_{\beta}(s,y)</annotation></semantics></math> from (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Scalar satisfaction samples (guarantee setting). ‣ 3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
We consider two standard forms:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{\tau}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{\tau}(s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}\widehat{V}_{\beta}(s,y)\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\widehat{\mathrm{RP}}_{\beta}(s,y)\leq\tau," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>RP</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}\widehat{V}_{\beta}(s,y)\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\widehat{\mathrm{RP}}_{\beta}(s,y)\leq\tau,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{\lambda}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{\lambda}(s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}\widehat{V}_{\beta}(s,y)-\lambda\,\widehat{\mathrm{RP}}_{\beta}(s,y)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>RP</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s)}\widehat{V}_{\beta}(s,y)-\lambda\,\widehat{\mathrm{RP}}_{\beta}(s,y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the penalty form is a Lagrangian relaxation.
If the feasible set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s):\widehat{\mathrm{RP}}_{\beta}(s,y)\leq\tau\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>RP</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y\in\mathcal{Y}(s):\widehat{\mathrm{RP}}_{\beta}(s,y)\leq\tau\}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty, we fall back to
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>, consistent with Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ F.1 Full pseudocode for DARC ‣ Appendix F Detailed decoding rules and baselines ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The constrained and penalized forms provide two equivalent ways to tune the reward–risk trade-off; their finite-candidate-set relationship is given in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS6" title="A.6 Constraint vs. penalty: Lagrangian view (finite candidate set) ‣ Appendix A Additional Theoretical Details ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Disagreement as a risk proxy: interpretation and limitations.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use disagreement as a human-centric signal of preference heterogeneity, and implement risk sensitivity through the KL-robust entropic value (<a href="#S4.E19" title="Equation 19 ‣ Risk-sensitive decoding (primary rule). ‣ 4.1 From preference maximization to risk controls ‣ 4 Disagreement-Aware Risk-Constrained Decoding ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) (a standard convex risk measure).
The mean–dispersion form arises only as a finite-sample surrogate (via LCB pessimism) or as a <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-DRO special case, rather than being the defining principle of our method.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, we do <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> claim that variance is a coherent risk measure (e.g., CVaR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Artzner et al., 1999</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rockafellar et al., 2000</a>)</cite> or that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">equivalent</em> to a tail-risk functional in full generality.
Instead, §<a href="#S3" title="3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that penalizing <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> arises naturally from maximizing a statistically justified <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lower confidence bound</em> (LCB) on <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(s,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(s,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding a principled <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pessimistic</em> selection rule under finite data.
Moreover, §<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Distributional risk: DRO characterizations of pessimistic value ‣ 3 Guarantees via Lower Confidence Bounds ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> provides a complementary <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distributionally robust optimization</em> characterization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wiesemann et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rahimian and Mehrotra, 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:162.9pt;vertical-align:-80.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-225.3pt,106.4pt) scale(0.433668424733865,0.433668424733865) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.45pt 2.5pt;" rowspan="3"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Type</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.45pt 2.5pt;" rowspan="3"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Method</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.45pt 2.5pt;" colspan="8"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.45pt 2.5pt;" colspan="8"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.3.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 2.5pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">MT-Bench</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 2.5pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">AlpacaEval 2.0</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 2.5pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">MT-Bench</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 2.5pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">AlpacaEval 2.0</span></td></tr>
