<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span><span id="S5.T3.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-scorer robust decoding.</span>
DARC variants vs. Base (mean Best-of-<math id="S5.T3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>).
<math id="S5.T3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>: per-prompt improvement over Base, aggregated across scorers (mean / min / mean<math id="S5.T3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>std); W/T/L is on aggregated <math id="S5.T3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
High-<math id="S5.T3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> uses the baseline disagreement proxy.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scaling robustness.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess scale robustness, we additionally evaluate our inference-time decoding rules on a stronger generator, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct</span>, using the same candidate-pool protocol and metrics.
We observe consistent improvements over mean Best-of-<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in risk-sensitive criteria (Tradeoff and prompt-level CVaR), with gains again concentrating on high-disagreement prompts (Table <a href="#A8.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Results. ‣ H.12 Additional Scalable Results: Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct ‣ Appendix H Experiments ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hyperparameter sensitivity and ablations.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Metrics (mean, risk, and tail). ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup and Implementation Details ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> summarizes a sensitivity study over the main knobs of DARC-<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, reporting improvements over mean Best-of-<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathrm{Tradeoff}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Tradeoff</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathrm{Tradeoff}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathrm{CVaR}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>CVaR</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathrm{CVaR}</annotation></semantics></math> on fixed candidate pools.
Increasing the candidate pool size <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> improves both metrics with diminishing returns (Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Metrics (mean, risk, and tail). ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup and Implementation Details ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>a).
The risk sensitivity <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> in the entropic robust value <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{V}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{V}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> yields rapid robustness gains that plateau at moderate values (Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Metrics (mean, risk, and tail). ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup and Implementation Details ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>b).
Fixing <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, enlarging the near-optimal set via <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> further improves robustness up to saturation (with slight degradation when overly permissive) (Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Metrics (mean, risk, and tail). ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup and Implementation Details ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>c).
Finally, the perturbation budget <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{aug}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>aug</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{aug}}</annotation></semantics></math> used to estimate proxy disagreement <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> saturates quickly; a small budget (e.g., <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>) suffices, keeping inference overhead modest (Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Metrics (mean, risk, and tail). ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup and Implementation Details ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>d).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We cast decoding-time alignment under heterogeneous preferences as risk-constrained decision making.
DARC uses a KL-robust (entropic) decoding rule with a conservative LCB/DRO interpretation, improving lower-tail outcomes while preserving mean quality.
Limitations include a finite candidate pool and scorer bias; perturbation-based disagreement proxies are scalable screening signals, not calibrated human-disagreement estimates.
Future work includes richer robustness signals and user/group-conditional risk control.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Impact Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents work whose goal is to advance the field of machine learning.
Our contributions focus on inference-time response selection under preference heterogeneity.
As with many machine learning methods, the proposed approach may exhibit uneven performance if the underlying proxies or learned scorers encode dataset- or population-specific biases.
We encourage reporting sensitivity to key design choices and auditing proxy/scorer behavior when the method is used in practice.
Consequently, the method should be used and evaluated with appropriate care in deployment.</p>
</div>
</section>
