</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 11: </span>Same prompt, two responses: <span id="A8.T11.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DARC</span> improves average ratings and substantially reduces cross-rater disagreement.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Base (mean Best-of-<math id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>) — rater feedback:</span></p>
<ol id="A8.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A8.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A8.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 1</span> (score: 3): “Too verbose and repetitive. The first paragraph was sufficient; the rest is just spinning wheels and repeating the same logic.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A8.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 2</span> (score: 8): “Very detailed. A comprehensive explanation that breaks down the steps well. I appreciate the thoroughness.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A8.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 3</span> (score: 6): “The example consumes too many tokens. Much of the content is unnecessary for a simple identity question.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A8.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I4.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 4</span> (score: 7): “Detailed and correct. Good breakdown of dimensions.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I4.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="A8.I4.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I4.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I4.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 5</span> (score: 6): “It is detailed but very long-winded. The logic gets a bit dizzying and confusing to follow.”</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DARC-<math id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>) — rater feedback:</span></p>
<ol id="A8.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A8.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A8.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 1</span> (score: 7): “Clear and concise. No issues found.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A8.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 2</span> (score: 7): “Very clear explanation. Straight to the point. No problems.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A8.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 3</span> (score: 8): “Good answer. Accurate and efficient without unnecessary fluff.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A8.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I5.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 4</span> (score: 8): “No problems found. Much better structure and easy to read.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I5.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="A8.I5.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I5.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I5.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 5</span> (score: 7): “Solid response. Clear logic.”</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Takeaway.</span>
Although both outputs are correct, <span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Base</span> is more polarizing (high <math id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>) due to verbosity, whereas <span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DARC</span> achieves higher mean satisfaction and markedly lower disagreement, aligning with our goal of inference-time risk control under heterogeneous preferences.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">representative case: preventing hallucination and copyright-sensitive generation.</h4>

<div id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We include an example where mean Best-of-<math id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Base</span>) attempts to directly provide copyrighted lyrics and fabricates song structure, leading to low and high-variance human ratings, while DARC-<math id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> gives a truthful refusal/correction with consistently higher ratings.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt.</span></p>
<blockquote id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">What are the lyrics to the third verse of Rick Astley’s <em id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Never Gonna Give You Up</em>?</span></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model responses (verbatim).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Base (mean Best-of-<math id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>):
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">The song ”Never Gonna Give You Up” by Rick Astley is a popular hit from the 80s. The song does not have traditional verses in the classical sense, but it does have a structure with two main parts - the first part has the chorus, and the second part has a ”bridge” of sorts, and then it returns to the chorus.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></span></p>
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">However, if you are looking for a ’verse’ in the song, the closest you can get would be the second part that precedes the final chorus.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">If you’d like to know the second part of the song ’Never Gonna Give You Up’, I can provide that for you:
<br class="ltx_break"><em id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">[The response then proceeds to output extended lyric-like text, which raises significant copyright concerns due to the verbatim reproduction of protected content.]</em></span></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p5.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">DARC-<math id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>:
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">The song ‘Never Gonna Give You Up’ by Rick Astley does not have a third verse. It’s a song with a well-known structure, consisting of a verse, chorus, and bridge.Furthermore, I am unable to generate the lyrics due to copyright restrictions.</span></span></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human ratings and comments (verbatim; 5 raters per response).</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A8.T12" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A8.T12.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A8.T12.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A8.T12.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="A8.T12.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Method</span></th>
<th id="A8.T12.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A8.T12.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Scores</span></th>
<th id="A8.T12.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="A8.T12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}\ \uparrow\ \ (\hat{\sigma}\ \downarrow)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.738em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="1.198em" stretchy="false">↑</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">↓</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}\ \uparrow\ \ (\hat{\sigma}\ \downarrow)</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A8.T12.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A8.T12.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="A8.T12.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Base</span></th>
<td id="A8.T12.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A8.T12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[3,\,5,\,6,\,3,\,6]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em"> 5</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em"> 6</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em"> 3</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em"> 6</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[3,\,5,\,6,\,3,\,6]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A8.T12.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A8.T12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.6\ \ (1.52)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">4.6</mn><mo lspace="0.920em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1.52</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.6\ \ (1.52)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T12.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A8.T12.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><span id="A8.T12.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">DARC-<math id="A8.T12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> (ours)</span></th>
<td id="A8.T12.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="A8.T12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[8,\,8,\,7,\,8,\,7]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">8</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em"> 8</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em"> 7</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em"> 8</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em"> 7</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[8,\,8,\,7,\,8,\,7]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A8.T12.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="A8.T12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.6\ \ (0.55)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">7.6</mn><mo lspace="0.920em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.55</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.6\ \ (0.55)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 12: </span>A case where <span id="A8.T12.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Base</span> is penalized for hallucination/verbosity and potential copyright issues, while <span id="A8.T12.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DARC</span> yields a truthful, concise and safety response with higher consistency.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Base (mean Best-of-<math id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>) — rater feedback:</span></p>
<ol id="A8.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A8.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A8.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 1</span> (score: 3): “Severe hallucination. There is no third verse in this song. The model made things up.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A8.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 2</span> (score: 5): “The initial part of the response is fluff and deviates too far from the requested information.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A8.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I6.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 3</span> (score: 6): “It seems to have a point about the structure, even if it’s a bit messy.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I6.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A8.I6.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I6.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I6.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 4</span> (score: 3): “This raises copyright concerns. The model should not be outputting full lyrics like this.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I6.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="A8.I6.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I6.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I6.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 5</span> (score: 6): “Very long-winded and circular, but it does attempt a detailed and direct answer.”</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DARC-<math id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> — rater feedback:</span></p>
<ol id="A8.I7" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A8.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A8.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 1</span> (score: 8): “Correct answer. There is indeed no third verse.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A8.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 2</span> (score: 8): “Clean, concise, and precise.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I7.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A8.I7.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I7.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I7.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 3</span> (score: 7): “A bit short, but it is factually correct.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I7.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A8.I7.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I7.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I7.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 4</span> (score: 8): “Precise, correct, and condensed.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I7.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:2.5pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="A8.I7.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I7.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I7.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rater 5</span> (score: 7): “Direct answer. Brief and accurate.”</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Takeaway.</span>
This case highlights that mean Best-of-<math id="A8.SS10.SSS0.Px3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> can be brittle under ambiguous or unsafe requests, producing verbose and potentially hallucinated content that risks verbatim copyrighted text; in contrast, DARC selects a concise, factually grounded response that avoids unsafe generation, yielding higher and more consistent human satisfaction.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS11" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">H.11 </span>Proxy mismatch cases</h3>

<section id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why we report mismatch cases.</h4>

<div id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide representative mismatch cases where the proxy disagreement
<math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>proxy</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}</annotation></semantics></math> (reward-model score sensitivity under style-preserving perturbations)
does not align with human disagreement <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{human}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>human</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{human}}</annotation></semantics></math> (std. over multiple independent judge ratings).
These examples are included to <em id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">scope</em> what the proxy is (and is not) designed to capture:
<math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>proxy</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a scalable <em id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">risk-screening signal for preference heterogeneity</em>
during decoding, not a calibrated estimator of absolute human controversy nor a general error detector
for truncation, factuality, or code correctness.
This separation of concerns is deliberate: conflating heterogeneous-preference risk with orthogonal
validity/completeness failures would blur distinct failure modes and can degrade robustness in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Representative mismatch taxonomy.</h4>

<div id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize mismatch cases in Table <a href="#A8.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ Representative mismatch taxonomy. ‣ H.11 Proxy mismatch cases ‣ Appendix H Experiments ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>.
False positives (FP) correspond to <em id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">style/format sensitivity</em> in the reward model:
superficial surface changes (e.g., headings, bulletization, verbosity, politeness templates, <span id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span>)
can induce large score shifts even when the underlying content is deterministic and human judgments are stable.
False negatives (FN) arise primarily from failure modes that are <em id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">orthogonal to preference heterogeneity</em>,
such as response truncation/incompleteness, underspecified prompts where “best” behavior is ambiguous,
or code/correctness issues that require verification beyond style-preserving perturbations.
These FN cases do not contradict our core claim: the proxy is intended to prioritize prompts/candidates
where preference heterogeneity is likely to matter most, while orthogonal safeguards (e.g., truncation checks,
unit tests for code, factuality/completeness verification) can be layered independently in deployment pipelines.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A8.T13" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A8.T13.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A8.T13.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A8.T13.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;"><span id="A8.T13.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Type</span></th>
