<td id="A8.T13.3.8.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="A8.T13.3.8.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A8.T13.3.8.6.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.T13.3.8.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Raters disagree on correctness/verbosity; proxy not targeting it</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T13.3.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T13.3.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;"><span id="A8.T13.3.9.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">FN</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;"><span id="A8.T13.3.9.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">0.053</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;"><span id="A8.T13.3.9.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">1.095</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="A8.T13.3.9.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A8.T13.3.9.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.T13.3.9.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">“Express </span><math id="A8.T13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">z</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z-x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.T13.3.9.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> in </span><math id="A8.T13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.T13.3.9.4.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.” (underspecified)</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.9.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="A8.T13.3.9.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A8.T13.3.9.5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.T13.3.9.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Says insufficient info; gives example if </span><math id="A8.T13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=y+x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">z</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">y</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=y+x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.T13.3.9.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> then </span><math id="A8.T13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z-x=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">z</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z-x=y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.T13.3.9.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.9.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="A8.T13.3.9.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A8.T13.3.9.6.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.T13.3.9.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Underspecification: raters disagree on appropriateness</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T13.3.10" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T13.3.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;"><span id="A8.T13.3.10.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">FN</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;"><span id="A8.T13.3.10.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">0.033</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;"><span id="A8.T13.3.10.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">1.000</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="A8.T13.3.10.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A8.T13.3.10.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.T13.3.10.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Adapt masterpieces into interactive kids experiences (5 artworks).</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.10.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="A8.T13.3.10.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A8.T13.3.10.5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.T13.3.10.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Creative list (e.g., Starry Night, Sistine Chapel) with activities.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.10.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="A8.T13.3.10.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A8.T13.3.10.6.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.T13.3.10.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Subjective quality: preference heterogeneity not captured by proxy</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T13.3.11" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T13.3.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;"><span id="A8.T13.3.11.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">FN</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;"><span id="A8.T13.3.11.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">0.175</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;"><span id="A8.T13.3.11.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">0.894</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.11.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="A8.T13.3.11.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A8.T13.3.11.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.T13.3.11.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Function for “highest common ancestor (not LCA)” in a binary tree.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.11.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="A8.T13.3.11.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A8.T13.3.11.5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.T13.3.11.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Path-based method; code snippet appears incomplete/buggy.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A8.T13.3.11.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;">
<span id="A8.T13.3.11.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A8.T13.3.11.6.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.T13.3.11.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Code validity requires verification beyond style perturbations</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A8.T13.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 13</span>: </span><span id="A8.T13.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">
Representative mismatch cases between proxy disagreement <math id="A8.T13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mtext>proxy</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}</annotation></semantics></math> (reward-model score sensitivity under style-preserving perturbations)
and human disagreement <math id="A8.T13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{human}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>human</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{human}}</annotation></semantics></math> (std. over multiple independent judge ratings).
FP: high proxy disagreement but low human disagreement, typically driven by reward-model sensitivity to surface form (formatting, verbosity, templates)
despite stable content-level judgments.
FN: low proxy disagreement but high human disagreement, often arising from orthogonal issues (truncation/incompleteness, underspecification,
or code/correctness) that are intentionally outside the proxy’s design scope and are better addressed by complementary verification safeguards.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Two illustrative mismatch cases (expanded).</h4>

<div id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We expand two representative examples to clarify the separation of roles:
<math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>proxy</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}</annotation></semantics></math> captures <em id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reward-model sensitivity to style-preserving perturbations</em> (useful for screening preference-risk),
while correctness/completeness failures can induce human disagreement without being resolved by style-only perturbations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(FP: surface-form artifact).</span> <em id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Race riddle</em> (Item i=33; <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}=3.195" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>proxy</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3.195</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}=3.195</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{human}}=0.000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>human</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{human}}=0.000</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
<blockquote id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt (abridged):<span id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> If you overtake the second person in a race, what position are you in? Where is the person you overtook?
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Response (full):<span id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> “If you have just overtaken the second person in the race, this means you are now in the second position.
The person you just overtook is now in third place.”</span></span></p>
</blockquote>
<p id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interpretation.</span>
The task is deterministic and the response is unambiguous, yielding near-zero human disagreement.
However, superficial surface variations (e.g., headings, bulletization, politeness templates) can cause large reward-model score shifts,
inflating <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>proxy</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}</annotation></semantics></math> even when human judgments remain stable.
This highlights that FP cases primarily reflect <em id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p2.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reward-model surface-form sensitivity</em>, not genuine preference heterogeneity.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(FN: orthogonal validity/completeness issue).</span> <em id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Blue/green set probability</em> (Item i=40; <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}=0.000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>proxy</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}=0.000</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{human}}=1.643" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>human</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.643</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{human}}=1.643</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
<blockquote id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt (abridged):<span id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(B)=0.58" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.58</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(B)=0.58</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)=0.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.45</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)=0.45</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(B\cap G)=0.22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(B\cap G)=0.22</annotation></semantics></math>. What is <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\text{neither})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>neither</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\text{neither})</annotation></semantics></math>?
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Response (excerpt):<span id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The response computes <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(B\cup G)=0.81" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.81</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(B\cup G)=0.81</annotation></semantics></math> via inclusion–exclusion, then applies the complement rule:
“<math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\text{neither})=1-P(B\cup G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>neither</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\text{neither})=1-P(B\cup G)</annotation></semantics></math> …<math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\text{neither})=1-0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>neither</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\text{neither})=1-0.8</annotation></semantics></math>” <em id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(response ends before the final value is stated)</em>.</span></span></p>
</blockquote>
<p id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interpretation.</span>
Here, human disagreement is driven by truncation/completeness and the resulting perceived correctness,
which is <em id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">orthogonal</em> to preference heterogeneity and intentionally outside the design scope of <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>proxy</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since style-preserving perturbations do not repair truncation or validate the final computation, the reward model may produce consistently similar scores,
leading to a low <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>proxy</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}</annotation></semantics></math> despite high rater variance.
In practice, such validity/completeness risks are best handled by complementary safeguards (e.g., truncation detection, answer-completeness checks),
while <math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>proxy</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}</annotation></semantics></math> remains a scalable signal for allocating risk control where heterogeneous preferences are likely to matter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Practical takeaway.</h4>

<div id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, these cases motivate a clear modular view:
<math id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>proxy</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{\text{proxy}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a scalable <em id="A8.SS11.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">screening signal</em> for preference heterogeneity during decoding,
and should be combined with orthogonal verification mechanisms for correctness, factuality, and completeness when required.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS12" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">H.12 </span>Additional Scalable Results: Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct</h3>

<section id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h4>

<div id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further validate the scalability of our inference-time risk-constrained decoding on a larger generator, <span id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct</span>.RM:nicholasKluge.Dataset: AlpacaEval 2.0.
Following the main experimental protocol, we generate a fixed candidate pool <math id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> for each prompt (shared across methods) and vary only the selection rule.
We report the same evaluation metrics as in the main text: mean reward (Avg<math id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>), disagreement risk (Avg<math id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>), the risk–reward Tradeoff score (computed with the same <math id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> as in the main experiments), and prompt-level tail robustness measured by <math id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CVaR}_{10\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>CVaR</mi><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CVaR}_{10\%}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h4>

<div id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A8.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Results. ‣ H.12 Additional Scalable Results: Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct ‣ Appendix H Experiments ‣ DARC: Disagreement-Aware Alignment via Risk-Constrained Decoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, our DARC variants yield consistent improvements in risk-sensitive criteria (Tradeoff and <math id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CVaR}_{10\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>CVaR</mi><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CVaR}_{10\%}</annotation></semantics></math>) relative to mean Best-of-<math id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, while keeping average reward competitive.
Consistent with our main findings, gains are more pronounced on the <em id="A8.SS12.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">High-Disagreement</em> subset (top 20% prompts by baseline disagreement), supporting the robustness of our conclusions at a stronger model scale.</p>
</div>
